::STAT_U03_L07_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L07_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.8
Question:
A relative frequency is best described as what?
{
~A. A title in a table#A title is not a frequency.
=B. A part divided by a total#Relative frequency compares a count to the relevant whole.
~C. A residual from a model#Residuals are prediction errors, not relative frequencies.
~D. A guaranteed cause#Frequencies do not prove causation.
}

::STAT_U03_L07_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L07_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.8
Question:
What is 25/100 as a percent?
{
~A. 100 percent#This uses the total.
~B. 75 percent#This is the remaining percent, not the part.
=C. 25 percent#25 out of 100 is 25 percent.
~D. 125 percent#This adds part and total.
}

::STAT_U03_L07_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L07_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.9
Question:
A joint relative frequency uses which total?
{
~A. A row total only#That would be conditional by row.
~B. A column total only#That would be conditional by column.
=C. The grand total#Joint relative frequency compares an inside cell to the full table total.
~D. No total#A relative frequency always uses a total.
}

::STAT_U03_L07_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L07_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.9
Question:
A conditional relative frequency within a row uses what denominator?
{
~A. The grand total#That is for joint or marginal relative frequency.
~B. The other row total#Use the row being conditioned on.
~C. The column label#A label is not a denominator.
=D. The row total#A row conditional percentage divides by that row total.
}

::STAT_U03_L07_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U03_L07_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.BVM.10
Question:
Which comparison is fairest when groups have different sizes?
{
=A. Compare percentages within each group#Percentages account for group size.
~B. Compare only raw counts#Raw counts can mislead when group sizes differ.
~C. Compare only labels#Labels do not show size or rate.
~D. Assume the larger group is stronger#A larger group can have a lower percentage.
}

